uded in README for running tests and navigating the obfuscated modules?\\n7) Release logistics: Preferred branch/tag and commit message for the audit snapshot.\\n\\nOnce confirmed, we\u2019ll apply changes, run tests, and deliver the audit-ready repo.\u201d\\n\\nIf you approve this draft as-is, I\u2019ll send it to Emily. If you want to adjust any default recommendations (e.g., you want tests obfuscated), tell me and I\u2019ll incorporate that before we reach out.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content=\"That sounds comprehensive and well-structured. I agree it's important to get clear guidance from Emily. Let\u2019s go ahead with that draft message. I'll send it right away:\\n\\n---\\n\\nHi Emily,\\n\\nTo apply the watermarking with minimal disruption and keep the build/tests green, can you please confirm:\\n\\n1) Scope: Obfuscate only implementation Python files under the source package; keep tests, setup.py, and run_tests.py readable so discovery and packaging work without changes. If you require tests obfuscated too, we\u2019ll update the test runner to discover x*.py.\\n2) Mapping file: We\u2019ll keep the .watermark_map.json strictly internal (excluded from the audit repo) unless you instruct otherwise.\\n3) Directories: We will not obfuscate directories, only files. Renaming directories increases breakage risk with little extra protection\u2014please confirm this approach.\\n4) Watermark batch/version: Provide the batch ID/version string to include in README (and optionally a short header comment).\\n5) Redactions: Are there any specific names/strings to redact from README/configs/comments?\\n6) Auditor guidance: Any specific instructions you want included in README for running tests and navigating the obfuscated modules?\\n7) Release logistics: Preferred branch/tag and commit message for the audit snapshot.\\n\\nOnce confirmed, we\u2019ll apply changes, run tests, and deliver the audit-ready re